            <ArticleTitle>Coronary-coronary bypass grafting to reduce the risk of aortic atheroembolism.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>167-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/j.1540-8191.2009.00958.x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Coronary-coronary bypass grafting refers to making anastomoses between two segments of the same coronary artery or between different coronary arteries, and provides less &quot;touch&quot; to the aorta, which is important for the patients with severely atherosclerotic ascending aorta. In this report we represent a case of a patient with extensive atherosclerotic aorta, in whom a saphenous vein graft was placed between the acute marginal and the posterior-descending branches of the right coronary artery during an off-pump coronary artery bypass grafting surgery.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
